<%- include('_open') %>

  <%# §12 — three states, one page. %>

  <div class="measure">

    <%# The quoted mono label is printed by _open from `title`; this is the
        serif heading under it. Export carries the same pair below. %>
    <h1 class="d1 import-title">Import</h1>

    <%# ── STATE ONE: before ────────────────────────────── %>
    <% if (!job && !locals.supplement && !locals.askSource) { %>

      <%#
        Two ways in, said before either is offered.

        The screen used to open with a disclosure about a folder of 44 zip
        files — an edge case, expanded by default — and never said the one
        thing somebody standing here needs, which is where the file comes
        from. Somebody who has not exported their library before does not
        have a file to drop and has no way to learn from this page that such
        a file exists.
      %>
      <p class="auth-lede">
        Bring the file your last service gave you, or just type the books
        out. Both land on the same preview, and nothing is written until you
        have seen it.
      </p>

      <%# The token rides in the query string: a multipart body is invisible
          to the CSRF middleware, and one authoritative check beats a
          per-route one. %>
      <form method="post" action="/settings/import?_csrf=<%= encodeURIComponent(csrfToken) %>"
            enctype="multipart/form-data" class="drop" id="drop">

        <div class="drop-zone">
          <input type="file" name="file" id="drop-file"
                 accept=".csv,.tsv,.txt,.json,.zip,text/csv,text/plain,application/json,application/zip">
          <label for="drop-file" class="drop-label">Drop a file, or choose one.</label>
        </div>

        <%#
          The services are named exactly once, here, where knowing which
          files work is genuinely useful. Never in the heading, never on a
          button, never in the nav. A product that puts a competitor's name
          on its own furniture is advertising for them.
        %>
        <p class="field-note mono-s drop-note">
          CSV FROM GOODREADS, STORYGRAPH OR LIBRARYTHING · OR A LIST IN A TEXT FILE
        </p>

        <%# One form, one submit. A textarea and a file input in the same
            submission means a pasted list gets the same preview, the same
            mapping and the same confirm step, rather than a shortcut around
            all three. %>
        <div class="drop-or"><span class="mono-s">OR</span></div>

        <label class="field paste-field">
          <span class="field-label mono-s">TYPE THEM OUT, ONE A LINE</span>
          <textarea name="pasted" id="paste" class="paste-box" rows="7"
                    spellcheck="false" maxlength="200000"
                    placeholder="The Secret History by Donna Tartt&#10;Stoner — John Williams&#10;Gilead"></textarea>
        </label>

        <p class="field-note paste-note">
          A title on its own is enough. If you know the author, put it after
          <b>by</b>, a dash, or a comma, and the book will be found more
          often. Numbered lines and bullets are fine.
        </p>

        <button class="auth-action paste-go" type="submit">READ THE LIST →</button>
      </form>

      <%#
        ── WHERE THE FILE COMES FROM ─────────────────────

        Knowing what to bring is the whole difficulty of this screen, so it
        belongs on it. Open by default, because the person who does not need
        it loses one glance and the person who does would otherwise have to
        guess that the answer is behind a disclosure triangle.
      %>
      <details class="import-help" open>
        <summary class="mono-s">WHERE THAT FILE COMES FROM</summary>
        <div class="import-help-body">
          <dl class="import-files">
            <div class="import-file">
              <dt class="mono-s">GOODREADS</dt>
              <dd class="field-note">
                <a class="more-link" href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/import"
                   rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">goodreads.com/review/import</a>
                → Export Library. It takes a minute to build, then the same
                page offers <b>goodreads_library_export.csv</b>. That one
                file is the whole thing.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="import-file">
              <dt class="mono-s">STORYGRAPH</dt>
              <dd class="field-note">
                Under Manage Account, an export of your library. It arrives
                as a CSV, by email.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="import-file">
              <dt class="mono-s">LIBRARYTHING</dt>
              <dd class="field-note">
                Under Import/Export, export as CSV or tab-delimited. Either
                reads.
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p class="field-note">
            You do not have to know which one you have. The columns say where
            a file came from, and you are only asked if they don't.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <%#
        There are two different things called a Goodreads export and they
        share no format. Somebody who used "Request my data" is holding a
        folder of 44 zips, 40 of which are HTTP request logs, and the only
        way to know which four matter is to open them.

        Closed, now that the page leads with the ordinary path: this is the
        answer to a question you only have if you are already holding the
        folder, and the summary line is that question.
      %>
      <details class="import-help">
        <summary class="mono-s">GOT A FOLDER OF ZIPS INSTEAD OF A CSV?</summary>
        <div class="import-help-body">
          <p class="field-note">
            Then you used Request my data rather than Export library, and what
            arrived is 44 files. Four of them hold reading, and only one of
            those is your library. Drop them here one at a time, still zipped
            — no need to unzip anything.
          </p>
          <%# Four names in a list is still four things to choose between,
              and three of them do nothing until the first has been imported:
              notes, dates and quotes attach to books that are already here
              and cannot create one. So the library file is marked, and it is
              first because FILES puts it first. %>
          <dl class="import-files">
            <% files.forEach(function(f) { %>
              <div class="import-file<%= f.kind === 'library' ? ' import-file-first' : '' %>">
                <dt class="mono-s">
                  <%= f.file %><% if (f.kind === 'library') { %><span class="import-file-mark">START HERE</span><% } %>
                </dt>
                <dd class="field-note"><%= f.label %></dd>
              </div>
            <% }) %>
          </dl>
          <p class="field-note">
            The other three attach to books that are already here, so they do
            nothing until the library has gone in. Do that one first, then
            come back for them in any order.
          </p>
          <p class="field-note">
            Everything else in that folder is request logs, ad settings and
            recommendation data. None of it is about books.
          </p>
          <p class="field-note">
            One thing worth knowing before you start: that export carries no
            author, no ISBN and no page count, so books are matched on title
            alone. The CSV from Export library is the better file. If you can
            still get it, use it, and keep these four for the notes and dates
            the CSV has never carried.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    <% } %>

    <%#
      ── A SUPPLEMENT ──────────────────────────────────────

      Notes, dates and quotes attach to books that are already here. They
      cannot create one, so there is no shelf mapping to confirm and no way
      for this to double a library. What it can do is land a note on the
      wrong book, so every row is named before anything is written, and so
      is every row that will be skipped and why.
    %>
    <% if (locals.supplement) { %>
      <% var sup = supplement; %>
      <p class="auth-lede">
        <% if (locals.done) { %>
          <%= written %> added from <%= filename %>.
        <% } else if (sup.applied.length) { %>
          <%= filename %> has <%= sup.applied.length %>
          <%= sup.kind === 'notes' ? (sup.applied.length === 1 ? 'note' : 'notes') : 'dates' %>
          for books you already have.
        <% } else { %>
          Nothing in <%= filename %> attaches to anything here.
        <% } %>
      </p>

      <% if (!locals.done && sup.applied.length) { %>
        <ul class="import-rows">
          <% sup.applied.forEach(function(a) { %>
            <li class="import-row">
              <span class="import-row-title"><%= a.title %></span>
              <span class="mono-s dim"><%
                if (a.field) { %><%= a.field.replace('_at', '').toUpperCase() %> <%= a.at %><%
                } else { %><%= a.at %><% } %></span>
              <% if (a.text) { %><span class="import-row-note"><%= a.text %></span><% } %>
            </li>
          <% }) %>
        </ul>

        <form method="post" action="/settings/import/<%= jobId %>" class="mt-18">
          <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="<%= csrfToken %>">
          <button class="auth-action" type="submit">ADD THEM →</button>
        </form>
      <% } %>

      <%# The skipped rows are the honest half, and they are the reason this
          screen exists rather than a silent merge. %>
      <% if (sup.skipped.length) { %>
        <details class="import-help mt-18">
          <summary class="mono-s"><%= sup.skipped.length %> NOT USED</summary>
          <ul class="import-rows import-help-body">
            <% sup.skipped.forEach(function(s) { %>
              <li class="import-row">
                <span class="import-row-title"><%= s.title %></span>
                <span class="mono-s dim"><%= s.why %></span>
              </li>
            <% }) %>
          </ul>
        </details>
      <% } %>

      <p class="mt-18">
        <a class="more-link mono-s" href="/settings/import">IMPORT ANOTHER FILE →</a>
      </p>
    <% } %>

    <%# ── The one branch that asks ─────────────────────── %>
    <% if (locals.askSource) { %>
      <p class="auth-lede">
        <%= rowCount %> rows, but the columns aren’t familiar. Which is it?
      </p>

      <form method="post" action="/settings/import?_csrf=<%= encodeURIComponent(csrfToken) %>"
            enctype="multipart/form-data" class="auth-form">
        <fieldset class="choice-set">
          <legend class="field-label mono-s">FORMAT</legend>
          <% sources.forEach(function(s, i) { %>
            <label class="choice">
              <input type="radio" name="source" value="<%= s.id %>" <%= i === 0 ? 'checked' : '' %>>
              <span class="choice-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="choice-text"><span class="choice-name"><%= s.label %></span></span>
            </label>
          <% }) %>
        </fieldset>

        <%# A file has to be chosen again — a browser will not let a page
            refill a file input, and nothing was kept. Pasted text was never
            a file, so it rides along and does not have to be typed twice. %>
        <% if (locals.pastedText) { %>
          <input type="hidden" name="pasted" value="<%= pastedText %>">
        <% } else { %>
          <label class="field">
            <span class="field-label mono-s">THE SAME FILE AGAIN</span>
            <input type="file" name="file" accept=".csv,.tsv,.txt" required>
          </label>
        <% } %>

        <button class="auth-action" type="submit">READ IT →</button>
      </form>

      <p class="field-note mono-s mt-18">
        COLUMNS FOUND: <%= header.join(' · ').toUpperCase() %>
      </p>
    <% } %>

    <%# ── STATE TWO: preview. Never skipped. ───────────── %>
    <% if (job && job.state === 'parsed') { %>
      <p class="import-summary"><%= h.importSummary(analysis.counts) %></p>

      <%# A list arrives with no ISBN and no page count, so a book is found
          on its title and — when there is one — its author. Saying how many
          carry one is the difference between a good match rate and a
          mystery, and it is knowable before anything is written. %>
      <% if (analysis.list) { %>
        <p class="field-note paste-note">
          <% if (!analysis.list.withoutAuthor) { %>
            Every line has an author on it, which is the best case for
            finding the right book.
          <% } else if (analysis.list.withAuthor) { %>
            <%= analysis.list.withAuthor %> of these name an author.
            The other <%= analysis.list.withoutAuthor %> will be matched on
            title alone, so a common title may land on the wrong book.
          <% } else { %>
            None of these name an author, so they will be matched on title
            alone. A common title may land on the wrong book.
          <% } %>
        </p>
      <% } %>

      <form method="post" action="/settings/import/<%= jobId %>">
        <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="<%= csrfToken %>">

        <%#
          Their vocabulary on the left, ours on the right, and the arrow
          between them doing the translating in public. Letting somebody set
          this is the moment the product's own language introduces itself.
        %>
        <%# Their vocabulary needs a heading; a list has no vocabulary to
            translate, only a question left over. %>
        <div class="mono-s dim mt-34 mb-8"><%= analysis.list ? 'WHAT THESE ARE' : 'SHELVES' %></div>

        <% analysis.mapping.forEach(function(m) { %>
          <div class="map-row">
            <span class="map-from"><%= m.value || (analysis.list ? 'Every one of them' : '—') %></span>
            <span class="map-count mono-s dim"><%= m.count %></span>
            <span class="map-arrow mono-s dim">→</span>
            <span class="select-wrap">
              <select name="map:<%= m.value %>" class="bare-select">
                <% statuses.forEach(function(st) { %>
                  <option value="<%= st %>" <%= m.status === st ? 'selected' : '' %>>
                    <%= st === 'SKIP' ? 'DON’T IMPORT' : st %>
                  </option>
                <% }) %>
              </select>
            </span>
          </div>
        <% }) %>

        <button class="auth-action import-confirm" type="submit">
          Import <%= analysis.rows.length %> →
        </button>
      </form>

      <% if (analysis.counts.unmatched) { %>
        <p class="field-note mono-s import-unmatched-link">
          <a class="more-link" href="/settings/import/<%= jobId %>/unmatched">
            <%= analysis.counts.unmatched %> WITHOUT A TITLE →
          </a>
        </p>
      <% } %>
    <% } %>

    <%# ── STATE THREE: during, and after ───────────────── %>
    <% if (job && (job.state === 'running' || job.state === 'done' || job.state === 'failed')) { %>
      <%#
        One mono line, rewritten in place by public/js/import.js. No bar, no
        spinner, no percentage counting itself up — the number is the only
        thing that changes because the number is the only thing that moved.
      %>
      <p class="import-line mono <%= job.state === 'done' ? 'done' : '' %>" id="import-line"
         data-job="<%= jobId %>"
         data-state="<%= job.state %>">
        <% if (job.state === 'running') { %>
          IMPORTING · <%= job.done %> / <%= job.total %>
        <% } else if (job.state === 'failed') { %>
          IMPORT FAILED
        <% } else { %>
          <%= job.result.imported %> in.<%
            if (analysis.counts.unmatched) { %> <%= analysis.counts.unmatched %> without a match.<% } %>
        <% } %>
      </p>

      <p class="import-after" id="import-after"
         <%= job.state === 'done' ? '' : 'hidden' %>>
        <% if (analysis.counts.unmatched || (job.result && job.result.failed && job.result.failed.length)) { %>
          <a class="more-link mono-s" href="/settings/import/<%= jobId %>/unmatched">FIX THEM BY HAND →</a>
        <% } %>
        <a class="more-link mono-s" href="/shelves">YOUR SHELVES →</a>
      </p>
    <% } %>

    <%# ── §12 — export, on the same page ───────────────── %>
    <%#
      Import and export sitting together is the quiet part of §12 said out
      loud: "a product that makes leaving easy is one people trust enough to
      stay in." Somebody arriving from another service is checking for
      exactly this, and finding it one scroll below the door they came in by
      is a better answer than any copy about it would be.
    %>
    <div class="export-section">
      <div class="eyebrow mono"><span class="tag">EXPORT</span></div>
      <h2 class="d2 export-title">Export</h2>
      <p class="auth-lede">Everything, as JSON and CSV.</p>

      <% if (locals.exportLink) { %>
        <a class="auth-action" href="<%= exportLink %>">DOWNLOAD IT →</a>
      <% } else { %>
        <a class="auth-action" href="/settings/export">BUILD THE ARCHIVE →</a>
      <% } %>
    </div>

  </div>
<%- include('_close') %>
